1. When you use `freopen("Error.txt", "w", stderr);`, it redirects the standard error stream 
    (`stderr`) for the **entire program**.
    
2.  `Macro for debuggin a array` : 
		 1. ``` cpp 
		    void _print(vector <int> v1){
		    cerr << '[';
		    for(int i : v1){
		        cerr << i << ' ' ;
		    }
		    cerr << ']' << '\n';}
		   ```
		  2. ``` cpp 
			     #ifndef ONLINE_JUDGE
				#define debug(x) cerr << #x << " "; _print(x); cerr << '\n';
				#define debugv(y) cerr << #y << " " << x << '\n';
				#else
				#define debug(x)
			#endif
			```
			
		 3. ```cpp
		    #ifndef ONLINE_JUDGE
			freopen("Error.txt" , "w" , stderr);
			#endif
		    ```
		4. In step one we created a function for printing our arrays and in step two 
		we attached that with our macro and after #ifndef ONLINE_JUDGE and before #else
		we can define multiple macros and that will not work on online judge and in step 
		3rd we attached that with a outside file .
		
3. We can use any defined function with macro. As Macros are just a "find and replace" 
   tool. The C++ preprocessor simply copies and pastes whatever text you write inside
   the macro before the actual code compiles.


4. `Function Overloading` : You can create multiple functions with same name but different
    parameters as input and even different body when you will that function you compiler
    will handle and match correct function automatically by seeing parameter.
    
    
